WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.320
What does polynomial time mean

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:03.840
when numbers are in binary?

3
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.880
A decision language is in

4
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.120
P when some deterministic decider

5
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.300
has a fixed-degree polynomial runtime

6
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.960
in its input length. A

7
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.640
number’s value can be exponentially

8
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.120
larger than the bits used

9
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.900
to represent it, so counting

10
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.280
value-sized iterations can be deceptive.

11
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.660
An integer N in binary

12
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.420
uses about log base two

13
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.380
N bits. A loop taking

14
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.840
N iterations therefore takes exponentially

15
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.340
many iterations as a function

16
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.280
of that bit count. Writing

17
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.740
the same value in unary

18
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.320
changes the input length to

19
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.180
N and changes this particular

20
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.340
complexity comparison.

21
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.960
Trial division up to square

22
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.580
root N correctly tests primality,

23
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.260
but its number of divisions

24
00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.320
is not polynomial in binary

25
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.600
length. Arithmetic on the trial

26
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.260
divisors also has bit cost

27
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.060
rather than being literally free.

28
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.220
A slow algorithm does not

29
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.700
establish a lower bound against

30
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.820
all other algorithms, so it

31
00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:58.840
does not prove that the

32
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.820
language lies outside polynomial time.

33
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.000
For a unary input, reading

34
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.780
its N symbols already takes

35
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.360
linear time in its length.

36
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.300
Counting the symbols and using

37
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.460
binary work counters still permits

38
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.500
a polynomial implementation of the

39
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.400
same trial-division idea. Do not

40
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.120
report a sublinear total input-processing

41
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.460
time while silently skipping the

42
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.680
full unary read.

43
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.560
The tutorial subset-sum dynamic program

44
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.580
runs in time proportional to

45
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.080
the number of entries times

46
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.060
the target value. If that

47
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.580
target is written in binary,

48
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.080
it may be exponentially large

49
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.440
in its own bit length.

50
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.280
Such a pseudopolynomial bound does

51
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.260
not establish membership in P

52
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.040
for binary-encoded instances.

53
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.400
With n input numbers of

54
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.120
order n bits, the full

55
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.740
input may have order n

56
00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.340
squared bits. A target of

57
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.560
two to n then yields

58
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.760
runtime roughly n times two

59
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.000
to n, superpolynomial in that

60
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.100
total input size. Calling it

61
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.420
two to a linear function

62
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.540
of the total size would

63
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.760
be a stronger claim than

64
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:02.860
this example supports.

65
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.940
Some bounds that look unusual

66
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.380
are still polynomial. A fixed

67
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.040
power of log n is

68
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.160
bounded by a polynomial, and

69
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.560
n to one hundred times

70
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.920
log n is bounded by

71
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.280
n to one hundred and

72
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.980
one. In contrast, n to

73
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.500
log n has no constant

74
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.200
degree, and two to square

75
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.700
root n is also superpolynomial.

76
00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.620
The photo quiz includes n

77
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.960
raised to two plus five

78
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.680
over log n. For a

79
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.500
fixed logarithm base, n raised

80
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.800
to five over log n

81
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.500
is a constant, so the

82
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.260
whole expression is a constant

83
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.120
multiple of n squared. An

84
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.120
exponent containing n is not

85
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.640
automatically superpolynomial; simplify the expression

86
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.920
before classifying it.

87
00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.760
To prove a language in

88
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.260
P, provide an algorithm, show

89
00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.300
correctness and halting, and bound

90
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.580
every operation polynomially in the

91
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.040
complete encoding. This includes number

92
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.000
sizes and any output generated

93
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.580
during intermediate work. Exhibiting a

94
00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.100
nonpolynomial algorithm supplies no lower

95
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.940
bound against all alternative methods.

96
00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.080
Things to take note of.

97
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:07.620
Always name the input length

98
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.440
before manipulating runtime, distinguish value-polynomial

99
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.260
from bit-polynomial time, and include

100
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.920
input-reading costs. A superpolynomial upper

101
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.800
bound fails to prove membership

102
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.720
in P, while it also

103
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.180
fails to prove nonmembership; those

104
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.760
are two different proof obligations.
